WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.301
Picture a vet clinic's booking agent at its launch demo.

2
00:00:03.631 --> 00:00:07.805
The developer runs it once, the right appointment appears, and everyone applauds.

3
00:00:08.605 --> 00:00:11.520
A week later, one booking in three lands on the wrong day.

4
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.680
The same request, sent twice, takes two different paths.

5
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.886
Normal tests assume the same input gives the same output.

6
00:00:19.216 --> 00:00:22.814
Agents break that assumption, so the testing pyramid changes shape.

7
00:00:23.614 --> 00:00:26.339
At the base, unit tests check tools, parsers,

8
00:00:26.389 --> 00:00:29.175
validators, and routing, with no model at all.

9
00:00:29.505 --> 00:00:33.130
Above them, component tests replay recorded model responses,

10
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.151
so they stay fast and repeatable.

11
00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:40.530
Next, a small integration set runs the real model, with tolerant assertions.

12
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.887
At the top, statistical evaluation scores a golden dataset and gates releases.

13
00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:49.432
Push as much logic as you can down the pyramid.

14
00:00:49.762 --> 00:00:53.467
Save the expensive layer for what truly depends on model judgement.

15
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:58.654
Never assert exact output text, because a harmless prompt tweak will break it.

16
00:00:58.984 --> 00:01:02.078
Assert on properties: the right tool, valid arguments,

17
00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:04.523
a cited source, and staying within budget.

18
00:01:05.323 --> 00:01:07.087
Then run each case several times.

19
00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:11.889
Hard rules must hold on every run, and the success rate must clear a threshold.

20
00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:17.397
pass@k asks whether at least one of k runs succeeded: a capability measure.

21
00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:21.730
pass^k asks whether all k succeeded: a reliability measure.

22
00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.912
The clinic's demo was a pass@k result.

23
00:01:25.242 --> 00:01:27.314
Production agents are judged on pass^k,

24
00:01:27.364 --> 00:01:30.035
which would have caught the problem before launch.

25
00:01:30.835 --> 00:01:34.118
When an answer is wrong, the trace shows which stage failed,

26
00:01:34.168 --> 00:01:35.841
and each stage has its own fix.

27
00:01:36.171 --> 00:01:38.530
A wrong tool calls for sharper descriptions,

28
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.049
and wrong arguments call for a tighter schema.

29
00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:45.423
The clinic's trace showed the right call returning a bad result,

30
00:01:45.473 --> 00:01:48.028
from a calendar tool with the wrong time zone.

31
00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:50.889
So the fix belonged in the tool, not the prompt.

32
00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:55.906
Your golden set needs the cases that break things, not just the happy path.

33
00:01:56.236 --> 00:01:59.095
Add ambiguous requests, injected instructions,

34
00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:01.562
timeouts, and questions with no answer.

35
00:02:02.362 --> 00:02:04.988
Exam tip: never assert exact strings,

36
00:02:05.038 --> 00:02:08.605
and judge production agents on pass^k, not pass@k.

37
00:02:08.935 --> 00:02:11.445
Read on to build the pyramid, layer by layer.
